        <h2>What Is a Gas Safety Certificate?</h2>
        <p>A gas safety certificate is often called a CP12. Its official name is a landlord gas safety record. A Gas Safe registered engineer gives you one after checking the gas appliances in your rental home. It shows each appliance has been checked and is safe to use.</p>
        <p>The rules come from the <a href="https://www.hse.gov.uk/gas/landlords/index.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Gas Safety (Installation and Use) Regulations 1998</a>. You must renew it every 12 months. There are no exceptions for old homes, for appliances you rarely use, or for gaps between tenants.</p>

        <div class="blog-stat">
          <div class="stat-num">12 months</div>
          <div class="stat-label">How often every gas appliance in a rented home must be checked. Missing a check is a criminal offence, and landlords can be prosecuted.</div>
        </div>
        <CallStrip inline title="Got a plumbing problem now?" text="A real person answers 24/7. No call-out fee." />

        <h2>Who Needs a Gas Safety Certificate?</h2>
        <p>Every landlord in England who rents out a home with gas needs a valid CP12. This includes:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Private landlords renting to people or families</li>
          <li>HMO landlords in Liverpool (an HMO licence also needs you to show the council a valid CP12 when asked)</li>
          <li>Landlords who rent to housing associations or councils</li>
          <li>Holiday let and short let landlords with gas appliances</li>
          <li>Landlords between tenants (the CP12 must be valid when the new tenant moves in)</li>
        </ul>
        <p>If you own your home and do not rent it out, the law does not say you need a CP12. But a yearly check is still wise. Most boiler makers also need a yearly service to keep your warranty valid.</p>

        <h2>What Is Checked During a CP12 Inspection?</h2>
        <p>The engineer checks each gas appliance in turn. The check covers:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Gas pressure and flow to each appliance</li>
          <li>A leak test on the pipes and joints</li>
          <li>The flue, and that fumes leave safely</li>
          <li>Enough fresh air for each appliance</li>
          <li>Safety parts, such as flame failure devices and overheat cut-outs</li>
          <li>Signs of poor burning, such as stains, soot and black marks</li>
          <li>Every gas appliance you provide, such as the boiler, gas fires, hobs and cookers</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Each appliance is listed on the CP12. If an appliance is a danger right now, it is "Immediately Dangerous". The engineer makes it safe before leaving. An appliance with a fault that could become a danger is "At Risk". We follow the <a href="https://www.gassaferegister.co.uk/help-and-advice/is-my-appliance-safe/gas-appliance-safety-checks/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Gas Industry Unsafe Situations Procedure</a> on every check.</p>
        <p>We cover boilers, gas cookers and hobs. Got a gas fire? Tell us when you book. If you no longer need it, we can disconnect and cap it safely, so it no longer has to be on the certificate. If you want to keep using it, it must be checked by an engineer registered for gas fires.</p>

        <h2>The Legal Obligations Landlords Must Meet</h2>
        <p>The rules are clear about what you must do. Get any part wrong and you may break the law.</p>

        <h3>Annual renewal</h3>
        <p>You must renew it every 12 months. There is some leeway. If the new check is done 10 to 12 months after the last one, the new one runs from the old end date. So you can renew a little early and lose nothing.</p>

        <h3>Providing the certificate to tenants</h3>
        <p>You must give current tenants a copy within 28 days of the check. New tenants must get a copy before they move in. Not giving tenants a copy is a breach of the rules in its own right.</p>

        <div class="blog-warning">
          <strong>Missing a check is a criminal offence</strong>
          <p>Landlords who do not keep an up-to-date gas safety record can be prosecuted. Renew every 12 months, and give your tenants a copy within 28 days.</p>
        </div>

        <h3>Record keeping</h3>
        <p>You must keep a record of each check for at least two years. You may need to show it to the <a href="https://www.hse.gov.uk" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Health and Safety Executive</a>, your council or a tenant. We email it on the day of the check, so you always have a dated copy.</p>

        <h2>How Much Does a Gas Safety Certificate Cost in Liverpool?</h2>
        <p>Our gas safety certificate costs £100 for up to two gas appliances, usually the boiler and hob. Each extra appliance after two is £20. A gas safety certificate and boiler service together cost £199, which saves you £21. Many landlords choose this, as it covers both yearly jobs in one visit. All prices include VAT.</p>
        <p>Book 3 or more properties and each certificate is £80. See our <a href="/gas-safety-certificate-liverpool">gas safety certificate page</a> for full prices and booking.</p>

        <h2>HMO Landlords and Gas Safety in Liverpool</h2>
        <p>Liverpool has a lot of HMOs. You must show the council a valid CP12 when asked. It is a condition of a <a href="https://liverpool.gov.uk/housing/private-rented-accommodation/multiple-occupancy-homes/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Liverpool City Council HMO licence</a>. The council can ask to see it, and its housing team can inspect HMOs.</p>
        <p>HMOs often have many gas appliances, such as shared boilers, gas fires and hobs. We check every boiler, gas cooker and hob in one visit and email the certificate the same day. Gas fires must be checked by an engineer registered for gas fires, or we can disconnect and cap them safely.</p>
        <CallStrip inline title="Want us to take a look?" text="Call for advice or a price. Gas Safe registered, no call-out fee." />

        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
        <div class="pf-faq">
          <details class="pf-faq-item"><summary><span class="pf-faq-tag pf-tag-orange">LEGAL</span><span class="pf-faq-q">Is a gas safety certificate a legal requirement for all Liverpool landlords?</span></summary><div class="pf-faq-body"><p>Yes. Under the Gas Safety (Installation and Use) Regulations 1998, every landlord with gas appliances in a rental home must have a valid CP12. You must renew it every 12 months. Missing a check is a criminal offence, and landlords can be prosecuted.</p></div></details>
          <details class="pf-faq-item"><summary><span class="pf-faq-tag pf-tag-navy">COST</span><span class="pf-faq-q">How much does a CP12 cost in Liverpool?</span></summary><div class="pf-faq-body"><p>Our CP12 costs £100 for up to two appliances, usually the boiler and hob. Each extra appliance after two is £20. A CP12 and boiler service together cost £199. With 3 or more properties, each certificate is £80. All prices include VAT.</p></div></details>
          <details class="pf-faq-item"><summary><span class="pf-faq-tag pf-tag-orange">TENANT</span><span class="pf-faq-q">When must I give the certificate to my tenant?</span></summary><div class="pf-faq-body"><p>Current tenants must get a copy within 28 days of the check. New tenants must get a copy before they move in. We email it on the day, so you can send it on straight away.</p></div></details>
          <details class="pf-faq-item"><summary><span class="pf-faq-tag pf-tag-navy">FAIL</span><span class="pf-faq-q">What happens if an appliance fails the inspection?</span></summary><div class="pf-faq-body"><p>We follow the Gas Industry Unsafe Situations Procedure. If an appliance is Immediately Dangerous, we make it safe before we leave. If it is At Risk, we note it and give you a written repair price. We never start repairs without your say-so. We cannot issue a CP12 until all Immediately Dangerous faults are put right.</p></div></details>
          <details class="pf-faq-item"><summary><span class="pf-faq-tag pf-tag-orange">HMO</span><span class="pf-faq-q">Do HMO landlords need a separate CP12?</span></summary><div class="pf-faq-body"><p>No, it is the same CP12. But an HMO licence also needs you to show the council a valid CP12 when asked. It must cover every gas appliance you provide, and you must renew it each year. We check every boiler, gas cooker and hob in one visit and email the certificate the same day.</p></div></details>
          <details class="pf-faq-item"><summary><span class="pf-faq-tag pf-tag-navy">REMINDER</span><span class="pf-faq-q">How do I make sure my certificate never lapses?</span></summary><div class="pf-faq-body"><p>We send every landlord a free renewal reminder. We contact you when your CP12 is due. If you have several properties, we can keep track of them all for you.</p></div></details>
        </div>
      </div>

